        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Green sapphire is an iron-rich corundum in which the yellow of iron combines with a slight blue component due to the iron-titanium pair. It forms mainly in magmatic deposits: crystallised at depth, it is brought up by alkali basalts, as in Australia, Thailand or Nigeria, before erosion concentrates it in alluvial deposits. More rarely it comes from metamorphic rocks, as in Sri Lanka or Tanzania. Its hue ranges from yellowish green to fir green.",
            "Trigonal system. Crystals form bipyramids and barrels, often zoned in blue, green and yellow. No cleavage, parting planes; conchoidal fracture. Hardness 9; specific gravity 4.00; vitreous to subadamantine lustre; refractive index 1.762 to 1.770; birefringence 0.008 (uniaxial negative). Distinct pleochroism, yellowish green and bluish green. Inert under UV because of iron. The spectroscope shows iron lines near 450 nm. It is often unheated. Its hexagonal colour zoning is typical of basaltic sapphires.",
            "Australia: Queensland (Anakie, Rubyvale) and New South Wales, a major source of basaltic green sapphires. Thailand (Chanthaburi, Kanchanaburi), Cambodia, Nigeria and Cameroon. Sri Lanka, Madagascar and Tanzania (Songea, Tunduru) yield metamorphic green sapphires, often lighter. Montana (United States) also produces a few.",
            "A stone of prosperity, loyalty and harmony, said to foster understanding of others and serenity. It is associated with the heart chakra, the sign of Virgo and, like all sapphires, the month of September. Cleansing under running water; recharging in gentle sunlight or moonlight."
        ),
        "es" to GemDetails(
